<!-- ============================================================
     Threat Intelligence Detection Rules  - 2026-06-24 Batch 5
     Sources:
       - CVE-2026-34908/34909/34910: Ubiquiti UniFi OS triple CVE chain
         Added CISA KEV June 23, 2026; 3-day remediation deadline June 26, 2026
         CVE-2026-34908: Improper access control (bypass auth gating)
         CVE-2026-34909: Path traversal (read arbitrary files post-bypass)
         CVE-2026-34910: OS command injection (RCE via exposed API post-traverse)
         Affected: Dream Machine, Dream Machine Pro, Dream Router,
                   Cloud Key, UniFi Network Server (all lt OS 4.1.13)
         Chained in the wild: access bypass - path traversal - command injection
         Management ports: 443 (HTTPS), 8080 (HTTP inform), 8443 (HTTPS UI)
       - macOS ClickFix Terminal DMG Execution (AMOS delivery, June 2026)
         Attack: fake CAPTCHA page socially engineers victim to paste
                 Terminal command using hdiutil attach -quiet -nobrowse
                 to silently mount and execute Atomic macOS Stealer (AMOS)
         Suppression flags: -quiet (no Finder dialog) -nobrowse (no Finder mount)
         Secondary: curl downloading .dmg/.pkg from shell, exec from /Volumes/
         C2 IOCs: svs-verificationdate[.]beer, hgodpcx.ajb8[.]com, 91.92.242.30
         Unit 42 + BleepingComputer, June 23, 2026
       - CVE-2025-67038: Lantronix EDS5000 OS Command Injection
         Added CISA KEV June 23, 2026; 3-day remediation deadline June 26, 2026
         OT device server; command injection via username parameter
         Unauthenticated root RCE against management web interface
       - CVE-2026-25253: OpenClaw ClawHub AI Skill Marketplace 1-click RCE (CVSS 8.8)
         ClawHavoc campaign: supply chain compromise of AI skill marketplace
         Malicious skills install AMOS; also delivers ClawHavoc backdoor
         C2 IP: 91.92.242.30 (shared with AMOS ClickFix campaign)
     Rule IDs: 103558-103583
     Author: SecOps Purple Team
     Date: 2026-06-24

     MITRE ATT&CK Coverage:
       T1190        Exploit Public-Facing Application (UniFi CVEs, Lantronix CVE, OpenClaw CVE)
       T1059.004    Unix Shell (UniFi command injection payload execution)
       T1204.002    User Execution: Malicious File (macOS ClickFix DMG mount)
       T1059.004    Unix Shell (macOS Terminal paste attack vector)
       T1071.001    Application Layer Protocol: Web (C2 via HTTP)
       T1195.002    Supply Chain Compromise: Software Supply Chain (OpenClaw ClawHavoc)
     ============================================================ -->


<!-- ==============================================================
     SECTION 1: CVE-2026-34908/34909/34910  - Ubiquiti UniFi OS Exploitation
     T1190 (Exploit Public-Facing Application) / T1059.004 (Unix Shell)
     CISA KEV  - active exploitation as of June 23, 2026

     Attack chain:
       1) CVE-2026-34908  - bypass access control gate on management API
       2) CVE-2026-34909  - traverse path to read /etc/passwd, SSH keys, tokens
       3) CVE-2026-34910  - inject OS command via now-authenticated API endpoint

     Detection approach:
       - Suricata IDS: alert on path traversal patterns in HTTP to mgmt ports
       - Suricata IDS: alert on command injection metacharacters in POST bodies
       - Frequency rule: rapid probe bursts against UniFi management ports
       - Correlated chain: path traversal alert from same src followed by
         command injection alert from same src (chained exploitation)

     Note: UniFi management ports are 8080 (HTTP inform), 8443 (HTTPS UI),
           and 443 (HTTPS on Dream Machine). The USW Pro XG 8 PoE + UniFi
           Network Application in this deployment use CEF syslog to Wazuh;
           network-layer detection comes from OPNsense/Suricata IDS.
     ============================================================== -->

<group name="threat_intel_2026_06_24,initial_access,exploit_pubfacing,ubiquiti_unifi,execution,macos_clickfix,ot_device,supply_chain,">

  <!-- ================================================================
       Rule 103558  - Suricata: path traversal attempt to UniFi management port
       Suricata signature matches http.uri containing ../ or %2F..%2F
       targeting destination ports 8080 or 8443 (UniFi management).
       Level 12: active CISA KEV; path traversal to IoT appliance is high signal.
       ================================================================ -->
  <rule id="103558" level="12">
    <if_sid>86601</if_sid>
    <field name="alert.signature" type="pcre2">(?i)(path.?trav|directory.?trav|CVE-2026-34909|ET WEB_SERVER.*(\.\.\/|%2F\.\.|%252F))</field>
    <field name="alert.metadata.affected_product" type="pcre2">(?i)UniFi|ubiquiti</field>
    <description>Suricata: Path traversal attempt against Ubiquiti UniFi management - CVE-2026-34909 (T1190)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1190</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>ids,suricata,path_traversal,ubiquiti_unifi,cisa_kev,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ================================================================
       Rule 103559  - Suricata: generic path traversal to UniFi mgmt ports (8080/8443)
       When a Suricata HTTP alert fires on ports 8080 or 8443 with traversal
       patterns in the signature but no explicit UniFi metadata tag yet.
       Broader catch for custom Suricata rulesets that may not yet carry metadata.
       Level 11: slightly lower confidence than explicit UniFi hit above.
       ================================================================ -->
  <rule id="103559" level="11">
    <if_sid>86601</if_sid>
    <field name="alert.signature" type="pcre2">(?i)(path.?trav|\.\.\/|%2F\.\.%2F|%252F|directory.?trav)</field>
    <field name="alert.dest_port" type="pcre2">^(8080|8443)$</field>
    <description>Suricata: Path traversal targeting port $(alert.dest_port) - possible UniFi CVE-2026-34909 exploitation (T1190)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1190</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>ids,suricata,path_traversal,ubiquiti_unifi,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ================================================================
       Rule 103560  - Suricata: OS command injection in POST to UniFi management
       CVE-2026-34910 delivers OS commands via POST body parameters.
       Suricata alerts on semicolons, backticks, $() command substitution,
       and pipe metacharacters URL-encoded or raw in POST request bodies.
       Level 13: command injection is highest severity  - direct RCE indicator.
       ================================================================ -->
  <rule id="103560" level="13">
    <if_sid>86601</if_sid>
    <field name="alert.signature" type="pcre2">(?i)(cmd.?inject|command.?inject|CVE-2026-34910|ET WEB_SERVER.*(;|\||%3B|%7C|%60|%24%28))</field>
    <field name="alert.dest_port" type="pcre2">^(443|8080|8443)$</field>
    <description>Suricata: OS command injection POST to management port $(alert.dest_port) - CVE-2026-34910 UniFi RCE (T1190/T1059.004)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1190</id>
      <id>T1059.004</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>ids,suricata,command_injection,ubiquiti_unifi,cisa_kev,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ================================================================
       Rule 103561  - Suricata: CVE-2026-34908 access control bypass probe
       Access control bypass (CVE-2026-34908) typically appears as requests
       to restricted API endpoints returning 200 that should return 401/403.
       Signature alerts on access-control-bypass or specific API paths exposed.
       Level 12: precursor to the full chain; high confidence of targeted attack.
       ================================================================ -->
  <rule id="103561" level="12">
    <if_sid>86601</if_sid>
    <field name="alert.signature" type="pcre2">(?i)(CVE-2026-34908|access.?control.?bypass|auth.?bypass.*(unifi|ubiquiti))</field>
    <description>Suricata: Ubiquiti UniFi OS access control bypass probe - CVE-2026-34908 (T1190)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1190</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>ids,suricata,access_bypass,ubiquiti_unifi,cisa_kev,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ================================================================
       Rule 103562  - Rapid probe burst targeting UniFi management ports
       Automated exploitation tools (Nuclei, Metasploit modules) send
       rapid sequential requests to test all three CVEs in the chain.
       20+ Suricata alerts from the same source IP in 60 seconds against
       management ports 8080/8443 indicates automated scanning.
       Level 13: burst scanning against CISA KEV target is critical.
       ================================================================ -->
  <rule id="103562" level="13" frequency="20" timeframe="60">
    <if_matched_sid>103559</if_matched_sid>
    <same_srcip></same_srcip>
    <description>Rapid path traversal burst (20+ in 60s) from $(srcip) against UniFi management - automated CVE-2026-34908/34909 exploitation (T1190)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1190</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>ids,suricata,path_traversal,ubiquiti_unifi,cisa_kev,automated_exploit,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ================================================================
       Rule 103563  - Correlated chain: path traversal then command injection
       The CVE-2026-34908/34909/34910 chain almost always sees path traversal
       (read credentials/tokens) followed by command injection from the same
       source IP within minutes. This correlation rule fires on that sequence.
       Same source IP hits 103559 then 103560 within 5 minutes.
       Level 15 (maximum): confirmed exploitation chain in progress.
       ================================================================ -->
  <rule id="103563" level="15" frequency="1" timeframe="300">
    <if_matched_sid>103560</if_matched_sid>
    <same_srcip></same_srcip>
    <if_sid>103559</if_sid>
    <description>CRITICAL: UniFi OS exploitation chain detected from $(srcip) - path traversal followed by command injection (CVE-2026-34909 -> CVE-2026-34910) (T1190)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1190</id>
      <id>T1059.004</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>ids,suricata,ubiquiti_unifi,cisa_kev,exploit_chain,critical,</group>
  </rule>



<!-- ==============================================================
     SECTION 2: macOS ClickFix Terminal DMG Execution  - AMOS Delivery
     T1204.002 (User Execution: Malicious File) / T1059.004 (Unix Shell)
     Unit 42 June 23, 2026  - AMOS stealer via hdiutil -quiet -nobrowse

     Detection approach:
       - macOS endpoint agent (Wazuh): process exec events via macOS audit
       - Match hdiutil with suppression flags from interactive shell parents
       - Match curl downloading .dmg/.pkg from shell parent
       - Match process execution from /Volumes/ paths (mounted DMG content)
       - Network IOC: DNS/HTTP to AMOS C2 domains and IPs

     Log source: macOS Wazuh agent using macOS Unified Log / auditd bridge
     Parent process field: data.audit.ppid_name or data.process.parent.name
     Process field: data.audit.command or data.process.name
     ============================================================== -->

  <!-- ================================================================
       Rule 103564  - macOS: hdiutil attach with -quiet flag from shell parent
       hdiutil attach is used legitimately by CI/CD pipelines but almost
       never invoked interactively from bash/zsh/sh/Terminal with -quiet.
       -quiet flag suppresses the normal Finder disk-mounted notification.
       Level 11: strong indicator; single flag from interactive shell is suspicious.
       ================================================================ -->
  <rule id="103564" level="11">
    <decoded_as>json</decoded_as>
    <field name="process.name" type="pcre2">^hdiutil$</field>
    <field name="process.args" type="pcre2">attach.*-quiet|-quiet.*attach</field>
    <field name="process.parent.name" type="pcre2">^(bash|zsh|sh|Terminal|osascript|python3?|ruby)$</field>
    <description>macOS: hdiutil attach -quiet from interactive shell parent $(process.parent.name) - ClickFix AMOS DMG delivery step 1 (T1204.002)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1204.002</id>
      <id>T1059.004</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>macos,clickfix,amos,hdiutil,t1204_002,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ================================================================
       Rule 103565  - macOS: hdiutil attach with -nobrowse flag from shell parent
       -nobrowse prevents the mounted DMG from appearing in Finder's sidebar.
       Combined with -quiet this hides the disk mount completely from the user.
       Level 11: same confidence tier as 103564 for single-flag detection.
       ================================================================ -->
  <rule id="103565" level="11">
    <decoded_as>json</decoded_as>
    <field name="process.name" type="pcre2">^hdiutil$</field>
    <field name="process.args" type="pcre2">attach.*-nobrowse|-nobrowse.*attach</field>
    <field name="process.parent.name" type="pcre2">^(bash|zsh|sh|Terminal|osascript|python3?|ruby)$</field>
    <description>macOS: hdiutil attach -nobrowse from interactive shell parent $(process.parent.name) - ClickFix AMOS DMG delivery (T1204.002)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1204.002</id>
      <id>T1059.004</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>macos,clickfix,amos,hdiutil,t1204_002,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ================================================================
       Rule 103566  - macOS: hdiutil attach with BOTH -quiet AND -nobrowse
       Both suppression flags together is the exact ClickFix pattern documented
       by Unit 42. No legitimate interactive use case combines these two flags
       from a shell parent. This is the highest-confidence single-event rule.
       Level 13: near-certain ClickFix attack indicator.
       ================================================================ -->
  <rule id="103566" level="13">
    <if_sid>103564</if_sid>
    <field name="process.args" type="pcre2">-nobrowse</field>
    <description>macOS: hdiutil attach -quiet -nobrowse from shell parent - HIGH CONFIDENCE ClickFix AMOS delivery (T1204.002)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1204.002</id>
      <id>T1059.004</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>macos,clickfix,amos,hdiutil,t1204_002,high_confidence,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ================================================================
       Rule 103567  - macOS: curl downloading .dmg or .pkg file from shell parent
       The ClickFix Terminal paste command typically includes a curl one-liner
       that fetches a .dmg or .pkg payload. Silent flags (-s/-L/-O/no-progress)
       are used to download without progress bars and follow redirects.
       Level 11: curl downloading installer formats from Terminal is suspicious.
       ================================================================ -->
  <rule id="103567" level="11">
    <decoded_as>json</decoded_as>
    <field name="process.name" type="pcre2">^curl$</field>
    <field name="process.args" type="pcre2">(\.(dmg|pkg)|\.app\.zip).*(-s\b|--silent|-S\b|-o\b|-O\b|-L\b)</field>
    <field name="process.parent.name" type="pcre2">^(bash|zsh|sh|Terminal|osascript)$</field>
    <description>macOS: curl silently downloading DMG/PKG payload from interactive shell parent $(process.parent.name) - possible ClickFix AMOS download (T1059.004/T1204.002)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1059.004</id>
      <id>T1204.002</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>macos,clickfix,amos,curl,t1059_004,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ================================================================
       Rule 103568  - macOS: process execution from /Volumes/ path
       After hdiutil attach mounts the DMG, the malicious binary is executed
       directly from the /Volumes/[dmg-nameto // path. Legitimate apps are installed
       to /Applications/ not run directly from /Volumes/.
       Level 12: execution from mounted disk image is very unusual.
       ================================================================ -->
  <rule id="103568" level="12">
    <decoded_as>json</decoded_as>
    <field name="process.executable" type="pcre2">^/Volumes/</field>
    <field name="process.parent.name" type="pcre2">^(bash|zsh|sh|Terminal|osascript|open)$</field>
    <description>macOS: process $(process.name) executed directly from /Volumes/ path by $(process.parent.name) - possible AMOS/DMG payload execution (T1204.002)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1204.002</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>macos,clickfix,amos,volumes_execution,t1204_002,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ================================================================
       Rule 103569  - Network IOC: AMOS C2 domain svs-verificationdate[.]beer
       IOC reported by Unit 42, June 23, 2026  - active AMOS C2 infrastructure.
       Matches Suricata DNS or TLS SNI alerts for this domain.
       Also covers shared C2 infrastructure used by ClawHavoc campaign.
       Level 14: confirmed active C2 domain from published threat intel.
       ================================================================ -->
  <rule id="103569" level="14">
    <if_sid>86601</if_sid>
    <field name="alert.signature" type="pcre2">(?i)svs-verificationdate\.beer</field>
    <description>Suricata: Connection to AMOS C2 domain svs-verificationdate.beer - ClickFix/AMOS stealer IOC (T1071.001)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1071.001</id>
      <id>T1204.002</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>ids,suricata,amos,clickfix,c2_ioc,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ================================================================
       Rule 103570  - Network IOC: AMOS/ClawHavoc C2 IP 91.92.242.30
       IP shared between AMOS ClickFix campaign and OpenClaw ClawHavoc supply
       chain attack. Any connection to this IP is high-confidence C2 traffic.
