    </mitre>
    <group>ids,suricata,amos,clickfix,c2_ioc,</group>
  </rule>


<!-- ==============================================================
     SECTION 3: CVE-2025-67038  - Lantronix EDS5000 OS Command Injection
     T1190 (Exploit Public-Facing Application)
     CISA KEV  - June 23, 2026; 3-day remediation deadline June 26, 2026

     Lantronix EDS5000 is an OT/IoT device server used in industrial and
     enterprise environments to connect serial devices over IP. The vulnerability
     allows unauthenticated OS command injection via the username parameter
     in the management web interface, yielding root RCE.

     Detection approach:
       - Suricata: HTTP requests to Lantronix management ports with cmd injection
       - Network scanning discovery: probe bursts against typical OT management ports
       - IOC-based: Suricata signature matching CVE identifier
     ============================================================== -->

  <!-- ================================================================
       Rule 103572  - Suricata: CVE-2025-67038 exploitation attempt
       Matches Suricata IDS alerts for the Lantronix EDS5000 command injection.
       Either explicit CVE match in signature or command injection patterns
       in HTTP POST to port 80/443/8080 from non-internal source.
       Level 13: OT device RCE  - high severity, CISA KEV active exploitation.
       ================================================================ -->
  <rule id="103572" level="13">
    <if_sid>86601</if_sid>
    <field name="alert.signature" type="pcre2">(?i)(CVE-2025-67038|Lantronix|EDS5000|lantronix.cmd.inject)</field>
    <description>Suricata: Lantronix EDS5000 OS command injection exploit - CVE-2025-67038 (T1190)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1190</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>ids,suricata,ot_device,lantronix,cisa_kev,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ================================================================
       Rule 103573  - HTTP POST with command injection metacharacters to OT ports
       Broad catch for OT management web interface command injection attempts
       when specific Suricata signatures for Lantronix are not yet available.
       OT management ports: 80, 8080 (HTTP) targeting non-server host types.
       Level 11: slightly broader; metacharacters in OT HTTP POST is still high signal.
       ================================================================ -->
  <rule id="103573" level="11">
    <if_sid>86601</if_sid>
    <field name="alert.signature" type="pcre2">(?i)(cmd.?inject|ET WEB_SERVER.*(username|user=).*(;|\||%3B|%7C|%60|%24%28))</field>
    <field name="alert.dest_port" type="pcre2">^(80|8080)$</field>
    <description>Suricata: Command injection in username parameter POST to port $(alert.dest_port) - possible CVE-2025-67038 Lantronix EDS5000 exploit (T1190)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1190</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>ids,suricata,ot_device,command_injection,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ================================================================
       Rule 103574  - Rapid probing against port 80/8080 OT management (scan phase)
       Before exploiting, attackers typically identify the target with a port
       scan or banner grab. 10+ alerts from same source to port 80/8080 in
       60 seconds targeting non-standard host types is anomalous.
       Level 10: reconnaissance phase for OT exploitation.
       ================================================================ -->
  <rule id="103574" level="10" frequency="10" timeframe="60">
    <if_matched_sid>103573</if_matched_sid>
    <same_srcip></same_srcip>
    <description>Rapid OT management port probe burst from $(srcip) - reconnaissance precursor to CVE-2025-67038 exploitation (T1190)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1190</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>ids,suricata,ot_device,scanning,cisa_kev,</group>
  </rule>


<!-- ==============================================================
     SECTION 4: CVE-2026-25253  - OpenClaw ClawHub AI Supply Chain (ClawHavoc)
     T1195.002 (Supply Chain Compromise: Software Supply Chain) / T1190
     Unit 42 June 23, 2026

     ClawHavoc campaign exploits CVE-2026-25253 (CVSS 8.8, 1-click RCE) in
     the OpenClaw ClawHub AI skill marketplace. A malicious skill, when
     installed with a single click from the marketplace, executes arbitrary
     code. The payload delivers AMOS stealer (macOS) or ClawHavoc backdoor.
     C2 infrastructure overlaps with the macOS ClickFix AMOS campaign
     (IP 91.92.242.30, domain hgodpcx.ajb8[.]com).

     NOTE: C2 IOC rules 103570/103571 above already cover the shared
           infrastructure. Rules below add CVE-specific and supply-chain signals.
     ============================================================== -->

  <!-- ================================================================
       Rule 103575  - Suricata: CVE-2026-25253 OpenClaw exploitation probe
       Matches Suricata alerts identifying exploitation of the OpenClaw
       ClawHub RCE. Covers both the CVE identifier and ClawHavoc campaign name.
       Level 13: 1-click RCE via AI skill marketplace  - high severity.
       ================================================================ -->
  <rule id="103575" level="13">
    <if_sid>86601</if_sid>
    <field name="alert.signature" type="pcre2">(?i)(CVE-2026-25253|ClawHavoc|clawhub.*(exploit|rce|inject))</field>
    <description>Suricata: OpenClaw ClawHub 1-click RCE exploit detected - CVE-2026-25253 ClawHavoc campaign (T1190/T1195.002)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1190</id>
      <id>T1195.002</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>ids,suricata,supply_chain,openclaw,cisa_kev,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ================================================================
       Rule 103576  - Suspicious AI marketplace skill installation from external source
       Wazuh HTTP log: POST to /api/skills/install or /marketplace/install
       endpoints with a non-trusted source domain in the payload.
       ClawHavoc bypasses normal marketplace signing by hosting malicious
       skills at attacker-controlled domains passed as source_url parameters.
       Level 11: unexpected external skill source URL in marketplace API call.
       ================================================================ -->
  <rule id="103576" level="11">
    <decoded_as>json</decoded_as>
    <field name="RequestPath" type="pcre2">(?i)/(skills?|marketplace)/(install|add|fetch)</field>
    <field name="RequestMethod">POST</field>
    <field name="RequestHost" type="pcre2">(?i)(openclaw|clawdeploy|clawhub)</field>
    <description>OpenClaw marketplace skill install API called - verify source is trusted (ClawHavoc supply chain T1195.002)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1195.002</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>traefik_api,supply_chain,openclaw,t1195_002,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ================================================================
       Rule 103577  - Successful skill install returning 200 from suspicious API
       If the marketplace install API returns 200 OK after 103576 fires, the
       potentially malicious skill was accepted. This is a post-installation
       confirmation that a response/investigation is warranted immediately.
       Level 13: successful external skill install against AI marketplace is critical.
       ================================================================ -->
  <rule id="103577" level="13">
    <if_sid>103576</if_sid>
    <field name="DownstreamStatus" type="pcre2">^2[0-9][0-9]$</field>
    <description>OpenClaw marketplace skill install SUCCEEDED (HTTP $(DownstreamStatus)) - possible ClawHavoc malicious skill installed (T1195.002)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1195.002</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>traefik_api,supply_chain,openclaw,t1195_002,confirmed,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ================================================================
       Rule 103578  - Process spawned by AI assistant/agent after skill install
       Post-exploitation: ClawHavoc payload is executed by the OpenClaw runtime
       process shortly after a malicious skill is installed. If a new process
       with an unexpected binary path is spawned by the OpenClaw agent process,
       it indicates skill-triggered code execution.
       Level 14: unexpected child process from AI agent runtime is very high confidence.
       ================================================================ -->
  <rule id="103578" level="14">
    <decoded_as>json</decoded_as>
